WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.965
In reality, could a car explode? Yes.

00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:08.420
But it probably would be more of a

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.570
little bit of an explosion

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.250
than more of just a
straight-up regular fire.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:13.750
Hello, my name is Tanner Foust.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.620
I've been lucky enough
to be a stunt driver

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.760
and race-car driver
for just over 25 years.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.990
I've had some world records,

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.300
including the longest jump
in a four-wheeled vehicle,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.230
the highest loop in a car,

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:27.080
and the fastest speed indoors.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.973
Today we're going to be
looking at car chases in movies

00:00:29.973 --> 00:00:32.345
and judge how real they actually are.

00:00:37.274 --> 00:00:38.730
That's one of the great things about sand,

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.900
is you can hit the
brakes and stop so fast,

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.330
as the tires dig into the sand almost.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.460
There's not a lot of quick response

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.550
other than braking in sand.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.050
So you have to really be ahead of the car

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.850
and kind of start your process of turning,

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.870
start your process of accelerating early,

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.320
knowing that there's going to be a delay

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.803
before it really happens.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.210
If they did have a driver in the car,

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:03.140
man, does that hurt.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.090
A lot of times what they'll do

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.370
is they'll have a cable
attached underneath the car.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:08.090
It simulates as if the bumper

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:10.020
hit something and flipped over,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.770
but really it's a cable that snaps,

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:14.360
and it really helps the
car do a front flip.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.130
I've done stuff like
that before on a cable,

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.080
and the shock is really quite aggressive.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.840
They have some tools that they can hook up

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.410
that will soften the blow a little bit,

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:24.785
but that looked like a hard one.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.540
There are a couple challenges
when you're driving

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.730
in the desert or driving
in sand like this.

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:32.710
One is the visibility.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.430
It can't be underestimated
how horrible it is

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.638
to lose complete visibility,
even for just a moment.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.984
These aren't really built

00:01:43.984 --> 00:01:47.551
and capable desert machines.

00:01:47.551 --> 00:01:50.190
They're really kind of
maybe put together for art

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:53.920
and also for some
hydraulics and some action.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.720
Sometimes even a Polaris RZR,

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.270
like a little buggy that's lightweight

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.910
with a huge amount of suspension travel,

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:00.900
can cover ground in the desert faster

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.370
than just about anything.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.286
Trophy trucks, which weigh about

00:02:04.286 --> 00:02:07.111
five times as much as a RZR

00:02:07.111 --> 00:02:09.930
but also have five times the horsepower,

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.720
are quicker in a lot of scenarios,

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.780
but usually smaller, lighter

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:15.413
with a lot of travel is better.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.950
But certainly the effects guys
had a handful with this clip.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.600
Pulling the top off of the car,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.860
once it's off and you're in the sand,

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:27.895
it's not like asphalt,

00:02:27.895 --> 00:02:31.150
where when metal hits
asphalt, it skips and slides.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.330
Once it hits the sand, that
would've been something

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.080
you wanted to steer completely clear of.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.500
And they would've had a lot
of safety built around that.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.220
Difficult to work in the dirt like that,

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:40.960
not just for visibility,

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.110
but if anything goes wrong

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:43.860
or when a car rolls in the dirt,

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.580
it's a much more violent
roll than the asphalt.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.850
A very small touch in the wrong place

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:49.780
could have a big injury.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:50.840
I'd give it an 8.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.040
That's a surprisingly dangerous scene.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.460
One of my favorite things
is to drive on the ice.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.130
You can actually have
a lot of control on ice

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:03.680
when you're going into it
knowing how slippery it is.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.350
You still have the same weight machine,

00:03:06.350 --> 00:03:07.890
still has the same horsepower,

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.050
the same brakes and everything,

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:10.890
but you have very, very little grip

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.880
connecting you to the surface.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.864
So you just have to really
think ahead on the ice.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.290
When the car flips over,
it's quite aggressive,

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:27.330
gets a very big pop and
a very big rotation.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.460
Believable that it would come
back and land on the tires,

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:33.333
not super believable that the
driver wouldn't get whiplash

00:03:33.333 --> 00:03:35.233
or his head wouldn't bounce
right out of the window.

00:03:35.233 --> 00:03:36.760
But the fact that it's ice means

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:39.140
that you're keeping your
speed the whole time,

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:40.723
even though you're sliding on your roof.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.610
In a straight line, you
can go very, very fast.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:46.890
80, 100 miles an hour.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:48.880
But for a corner that maybe you could take

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.220
40 miles an hour in the dry,

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.287
you may have to slow down

00:03:51.287 --> 00:03:53.520
to 5 or 10 miles an hour on the ice.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:01.360
That to me looks like my
version of Disneyland.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.880
The colder the ice is,
the more traction it has.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:07.590
Once ice gets closer
to melting temperature,

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.610
it gets more slippery once it
gets a layer of water on it.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:11.950
So the warmer the ice,

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:14.740
if you're going to use studs
or spikes on the tires,

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.750
the taller they would need to be.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.550
But it would be difficult to get traction

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:20.890
if they ever did have ice

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:22.970
with actually water on top of it.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:25.237
So I imagine it was some
sort of different surface.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.150
Those are massive spikes.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:34.040
So, in reality they would've
been a third that size

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.660
at the very most.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:38.320
If I had those spikes
ready to go in tires,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.080
I'd leave them out all the time

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.570
and just be digging away.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:43.420
I think a lot of the dynamics

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.960
of them sliding around on
ice were really beautiful

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.760
and done completely practically.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.135
So I'd give it an 8½ for realism.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:57.469
This brings back a lot of
memories, I'll be honest.

00:04:57.469 --> 00:05:01.660
Driving the 350Z right
there, the gray 350Z.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.540
Drifting is definitely not
the fast way through a corner.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.240
Certainly not as fast

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.410
as what a drifter would call grip driving,

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.370
which is what you normally see a race car

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:12.350
do through a corner.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:15.370
It's really an expression, or
an attitude, or a lifestyle.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.880
It looks faster on film in a lot of ways.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.800
When a car is sliding
and making tire smoke,

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.883
it kind of equates to power.

00:05:26.286 --> 00:05:28.170
All of these cars had
such different traction

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:29.170
and such different power

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:31.040
that getting them to match up together

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.520
was actually a little tricky.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.210
Bumps that you get in an urban setting

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:35.473
going through city streets,

00:05:35.473 --> 00:05:39.070
over manholes, over crosswalks and paint,

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:40.600
make it a little bit tricky,

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:42.690
but I was competing in Formula Drift,

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.000
as was another one of the
drivers that you saw there,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.475
so we could get the cars
very close together.

00:05:54.397 --> 00:05:56.040
That was, like, 60 miles an hour

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.590
in reverse in that 350Z.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.770
And that was one of the scariest things.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:01.990
It doesn't look scary at all,

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:03.130
but that was one of the scariest things,

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:04.900
is going backwards that fast over bumps,

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:08.100
the car can really easily
get chucked sideways.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.600
"Tokyo Drift" for realism

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.220
I think is a solid 9½.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:15.380
We loved bringing drifting onto the screen

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.821
as a bit of an underground
sport turning mainstream.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:25.220
Driving on narrow streets,

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.530
especially old European cities like this,

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:30.080
they have supersharp curbs.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:32.740
Any little mistake is a wheel broken.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:35.420
The driver has to be super
precise when doing drifting

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:37.290
in tight quarters like that.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:38.890
The car is very bespoke,

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:40.780
based on the Aston Martin DB5,

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:42.850
but the capability of the actual stunt car

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:46.570
needs to go higher than
the stock DB5 would be.

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:49.460
In this case, they actually
built a car from scratch

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:52.952
designed around the
stunts that it had to do.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:58.630
I know that they had to add
traction to the cobblestones

00:06:58.630 --> 00:06:59.820
because they were so slippery.

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:04.343
They actually spread a soda
over those to make it sticky

00:07:04.343 --> 00:07:06.410
and allow the car to have more grip.

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:08.430
It seemed to work, because
the car moves really well,

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.789
slides around, and goes real
fast on those cobblestones.

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:15.960
So, a car, to do that maneuver,

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:17.070
would've had a strong suspension,

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.750
possibly a skid plate in
case it did bottom out.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:21.250
When you come film in a place like that,

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.340
they would have to save the cobblestones

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:25.200
because those are historic things.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.220
So they would've had to make
sure the car was tall enough

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:30.640
that it didn't bottom out
and scrape those stones.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.010
And then it would've had
to have a hand brake,

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:35.430
kind of like the emergency
brake in your car

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.740
but much more powerful, so
that when you pulled it,

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.060
it actually locked up the back tires.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:42.760
And when the DB5 got to
the bottom of the stairs

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:45.110
and slid the back of the car out,

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.850
the driver would simply
lock up the back tires

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:49.960
to start the slide, let
go of the hand brake,

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.333
and go to the gas and then
have a nice beautiful drift

00:07:53.333 --> 00:07:54.880
down those cobblestone roads.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.260
Could you take a stock DB5

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:02.430
and drive it down those stairs? Sure.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.540
Could you do the doughnuts? Probably not.

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:07.200
In order to do actually a
traditional doughnut like that,

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.650
it needs a rear-wheel-drive car

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:10.720
with enough power to spin the tires;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:13.290
wheel speed needs to be
over 30, 40 miles an hour,

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.620
so that's over a couple
hundred horsepower;

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:19.110
and have some sort of
limited-slip differential

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.680
or something that's
between the two rear tires

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:24.640
that doesn't allow one tire to spin freely

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:26.048
and the other one not to spin.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.120
As the stunt driver,

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:32.370
it would've been a little difficult,

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:34.350
because once you are lost in the smoke

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:35.927
and having to drive out of it

00:08:35.927 --> 00:08:37.830
without crashing into people or cars,

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:38.930
that would've been tricky.

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:41.930
Taking away from the
machine guns in the car,

00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:45.150
just the driving itself,
it's done very practically.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:46.958
So I'd give it an 8 out of 10.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.370
You wouldn't really be able to flick it up

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:52.770
onto the two wheels.

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:54.640
You maybe could do that with a Jeep

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.210
or some sort of tall SUV with
a higher center of gravity,

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:00.940
but with a performance car
like this little Audi here,

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:02.330
you would need some sort of a ramp

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:03.863
to get it up on the two wheels.

00:09:09.179 --> 00:09:11.800
It looks like it would've
been tough to sell that speed.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:14.240
Skiing a car is not fast.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.660
And usually you're really only
doing 25, 30 miles an hour.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:20.140
There are guys that have
world records skiing cars.

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:22.556
Maximum speed they can do is in the 30s.

00:09:28.547 --> 00:09:31.370
It's really, really unstable up there.

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:33.740
The skill that the car needs to have

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:37.112
is it needs to not allow all the power

00:09:37.112 --> 00:09:39.980
to just be lost on those
tires that are in the air.

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:41.940
And then doing it this long,

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:45.930
you need to have fuel
pickup in the fuel cell

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:48.740
or in the gas tank that
is on the bottom side

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:49.935
so it doesn't just start

00:09:49.935 --> 00:09:52.060
sucking air into the
engine and kill the engine.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.130
If you look just after the truck,

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:56.250
you actually see the
car is kind of crabbing.

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:59.190
It doesn't really drive
perfectly straight when it skis.

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:00.570
It certainly is not easy,

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:03.840
and very, very easy to damage a car

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.749
just by going a little bit too far.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:10.470
Rally racing through the forest

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.650
is a big part of Audi's history, actually,

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:16.060
hearkening back to their old
Audi Quattro racing days.

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:17.010
It's all good.

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:23.810
I haven't seen a car take a drop like that

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:25.660
and then just explode spontaneously.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:28.650
If you had a stock car
and sent it off of a cliff

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:31.550
and a gas tank were to rupture,

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:33.010
then you could ignite it.

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:35.100
In reality, could a car explode? Yes.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:36.230
But it probably would be more

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:37.688
of a little bit of an explosion

00:10:37.688 --> 00:10:40.080
than more of just a
straight-up regular fire.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:42.240
This sequence doesn't
feel that real to me.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.430
So I would give it a 6 or 6½.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:47.450
Buddy: I got them, don't worry.

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:48.600
Being based in a video game,

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:50.760
I just love that they still had stunt guys

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:52.510
doing the real driving anyway.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.220
In real life, you could dodge

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:00.290
those kind of obstacles that quick.

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:02.710
It's just that getting the timing right

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:04.340
would be almost lucky

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:05.760
and then doing it more than once

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.280
would not be that possible.

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:09.560
So the speed of the car is very realistic.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.560
But the actual obstacles bouncing at him,

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:13.368
all of those were put in in post.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.850
Driving up steep hills
can be super tricky.

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:22.950
That's one thing you forget,

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:25.120
is when you're on a really steep incline,

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:26.528
you have gravity working with you

00:11:26.528 --> 00:11:27.430
when you go to the brakes.

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:29.460
It's working against you
when you're accelerating.

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:30.930
In this particular case,

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.580
I don't think they drove up a hill,

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:34.560
and all of the pitch
and incline that you see

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:37.851
is just done from camera angle
and from computer graphics.

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:46.800
That jump looked real to me.

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:48.520
Even the way the front bumper popped off

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:49.750
looked very realistic.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:52.810
I actually had to call
Brent Fletcher, the driver,

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:54.100
to see if the jump was real.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:55.500
He said they didn't jump the car.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.390
On realism, I'd give it a 6.

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:00.020
But because it is in a video game

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:03.309
and it's not supposed to
be real, I'll give it a 7.

00:12:03.309 --> 00:12:05.541
Benji: What are we gonna do?!

00:12:05.541 --> 00:12:06.612
Uh ...

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:13.210
I love the fact that Tom
Cruise and his hand movement

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:14.630
with the hand brake and the steering wheel

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:16.610
and where he's looking is all correct.

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:18.527
It's exactly what you would do in the car.

00:12:21.860 --> 00:12:22.826
Gun!

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:26.730
Newer cars, like the one in this clip,

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:29.650
usually have a speed limiter in reverse.

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:31.700
Somewhere around 20, 25 miles an hour

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:33.810
will be the maximum they'll
be allowed to drive.

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:36.090
But they would've done
that just to get some

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:39.510
of those drive-bys at a
realistic 40, 45 miles an hour.

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:41.760
Which in reverse is super, super fast.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:50.250
Hang on!

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:52.260
That makes me almost want to be sick.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.580
I don't think there was
a driver in that car.

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:56.340
That's a lot of speed to carry

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:58.670
that much momentum in the air,

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:01.340
probably over 50, 55 miles an hour.

00:13:01.340 --> 00:13:02.964
The risk is, when it jumps,

00:13:02.964 --> 00:13:05.090
if it rotates over and lands on the roof,

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:06.240
that's just too much of a risk

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:07.840
to put actually somebody in there.

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:09.310
I would give it an 8.

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:11.250
It gets a high score because Tom,

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:13.483
I know he did a lot of
the driving himself.

00:13:15.323 --> 00:13:16.156
Ah!

00:13:16.156 --> 00:13:19.472
Look at it!

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:26.180
The jump is ridiculously big,

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:28.890
and true to "Dukes of Hazzard" style

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:31.170
from the '70s and '80s,

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:33.170
all the actors do is just
a little head bounce,

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:34.080
and then they're fine.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:36.180
This was my first movie
that I ever worked on.

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:37.300
There was nobody in the car.

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:40.120
They had 26 General Lees to launch,

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:42.130
and they just hoped one of them flew

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:44.230
and landed in kind of a realistic way.

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:45.875
Jumping a car is pretty hard,

00:13:45.875 --> 00:13:49.260
because in the air you can't control much

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:51.090
about the attitude of the car.

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:55.080
Like, a motorcycle you can
pitch quite a bit up and down,

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:57.580
because the weight of the
tires is such a big percentage

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:01.150
of the overall size
and weight of the bike.

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:02.510
With a car, it's not like that.

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:04.410
You can control a little bit in the air,

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:07.290
but it's more about how
it takes off on the ramp.

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:08.980
The longer the ramp,

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:11.310
usually the better
chance you have of flying

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:12.960
with the nose kind of up.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:14.818
"Dukes of Hazzard," it was a whole team

00:14:14.818 --> 00:14:16.652
of stuntpeople putting this together,

00:14:16.652 --> 00:14:18.360
so everything was real.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:20.100
Occasionally there wasn't
somebody in the car.

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.758
I'd give it a 9½ out of 10 for realism.

00:14:25.587 --> 00:14:26.779
Bandit: That's not good.

00:14:29.770 --> 00:14:34.128
Pontiac Firebird Trans Am was
not ideal off-road at all.

00:14:34.128 --> 00:14:35.320
Just look at the exhaust pipes getting

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:37.100
peppered by rocks from the back tires

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:38.760
and hanging down behind the back tires.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:41.260
But it screamed America at the time.

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:50.900
That landing looked rough.

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:53.430
He just hits it, and
you can see in the shot,

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:55.160
that it looks like he comes up a bit short

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:56.580
and blows sand everywhere.

00:14:56.580 --> 00:15:00.390
They didn't have a lot of
technology built into the cars,

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:03.310
and they didn't hire race-car
drivers to do the stunts.

00:15:03.310 --> 00:15:06.700
They would sometimes just
have to say, "This looks good,

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:08.208
the wind is coming out
of the right direction,

00:15:08.208 --> 00:15:09.266
and just do it."

00:15:09.266 --> 00:15:11.020
So it was pretty gutsy, and it involved

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:14.900
a lot of just intrinsic
experience know-how

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:17.310
to have a long career in
stunts, which I respect.

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:18.460
That's a 10 out of 10.

00:15:21.593 --> 00:15:23.330
If you think you couldn't see in dust,

00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:25.520
that's the thing you don't even realize,

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:27.520
is that that is just completely blind.

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:29.340
No visibility whatsoever.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:30.760
I imagine that they had something

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:33.240
keeping it on a specific line,

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:35.590
and that line of stuff would not be

00:15:35.590 --> 00:15:37.023
solid enough to stop it.

00:15:42.041 --> 00:15:43.760
I think it's all completely practical,

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:45.600
the way that that car
dropped off of there.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:47.870
But the front tires are suspended in air,

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:50.380
and the only thing holding
the car up is the rear,

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:53.450
and the springs are pushing
the rear of the car up.

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:56.550
So that's why cars tend to nose over.

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:57.780
The slower you're going,

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:00.270
the longer the back
tires are by themselves.

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:02.753
The car was just going too
slow to really jump flat.

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:13.950
Realistic to do all that in one?

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:16.490
With that big drop where
the car probably endo'd?

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:17.640
Not very realistic.

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.620
There'd be an insurance
conversation to be had for sure.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:23.500
I think they had to piece
this one together quite a bit.

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:26.530
For realism, I'd give this a 9.

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:30.365
I think my favorite movie
car-chase scene, strangely,

00:16:30.365 --> 00:16:33.080
is "Tokyo Drift." "Fast and Furious."

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:34.680
Having been close to that one

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:37.668
and seeing how many stuntpeople it took

00:16:37.668 --> 00:16:39.792
to put together all
those scenes where you're

00:16:39.792 --> 00:16:42.760
in a car chase drifting
through oncoming traffic,

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:43.593
it was incredible, I think,

00:16:43.593 --> 00:16:46.070
what actually went out on-screen.

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:46.928
Thanks for watching.

00:16:46.928 --> 00:16:49.860
If you enjoyed this video,
click the video above.

